  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "D(p^k) \\;:=\\; 2p^k - \\sigma(p^k),",
    "plain": "A prime power $p^k$ is always *deficient*: the sum of its divisors falls short of $2p^k$, so it can never be a perfect number. This problem asks precisely *how far short* it falls. The parent proof shows only that the gap is positive; here we pin down its exact size, $2p^k - \\sigma(p^k) = p^k - (1 + p + \\cdots + p^{k-1})$, and describe how it behaves as the prime or the exponent grows. For $p = 2$ the gap is always exactly $1$ (powers of two are only *just* deficient), but for larger primes the gap blows up like $p^k \\cdot \\frac{p-2}{p-1}$. Equivalently, the \"abundancy\" $\\sigma(p^k)/p^k$ — the divisor sum measured in units of $p^k$ — climbs toward a hard ceiling of $p/(p-1)$ that it never reaches.",
